= Senescence — Simple =

'''Senescence''' is used for age-related biological decline and, in cell biology, for a particular state in which a cell enters a long-lasting growth arrest. These meanings are connected but are not the same thing.

== Organismal senescence ==

At the organism level, senescence describes changes with age that can reduce survival, fertility, repair capacity, or physiological resilience.

Aging does not proceed at one universal rate. Different species, populations, organs, and individuals show different patterns. Some organisms show strong increases in mortality with age, while others show much weaker actuarial senescence.

The Gompertz model is often used to describe an approximately exponential rise in adult mortality with age, but it is a demographic model rather than a universal law of aging.

== Why aging evolves ==

Evolutionary biology offers several compatible explanations for why late-life deterioration can persist.

'''Mutation accumulation''' proposes that harmful effects expressed late in life are weakly removed by natural selection.

'''Antagonistic pleiotropy''' proposes that a genetic effect can be favored because it improves early-life survival or reproduction even if it causes later-life harm.

'''Disposable soma''' theory emphasizes tradeoffs between reproduction and long-term maintenance.

These theories address why aging can evolve. They do not identify one molecular cause of aging.

== Cellular senescence ==

A proliferating cell can enter a durable state in which it usually stops dividing while remaining metabolically active.

Senescence can be triggered by:

* DNA damage;
* telomere dysfunction;
* oncogene activation;
* oxidative or other cellular stress.

Important pathways often involve p53–p21 and p16–RB signaling.

Cellular senescence can be protective. Preventing a damaged cell from continuing to divide can suppress cancer, and senescent cells can participate in development and wound repair.

Persistent accumulation of senescent cells can also contribute to tissue dysfunction and chronic inflammation.

== No single senescence marker ==

Researchers cannot identify every senescent cell with one universal test.

Useful evidence can include:

* persistent loss of proliferation;
* senescence-associated β-galactosidase activity;
* p16 or p21 expression in the appropriate context;
* DNA-damage features;
* changes in cell shape or chromatin;
* a senescence-associated secretory phenotype.

Consensus guidance recommends combining multiple features and appropriate controls rather than treating one marker as definitive.<ref name="gorgoulis">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2019.10.005 Gorgoulis, V. et al. (2019), “Cellular Senescence: Defining a Path Forward,” ''Cell'' 179, 813–827.]</ref>

== Hallmarks of aging ==

A widely used framework organizes aging research into interacting “hallmarks.” The 2023 update describes twelve hallmarks, including genomic instability, telomere attrition, epigenetic alteration, loss of proteostasis, disabled macroautophagy, deregulated nutrient sensing, mitochondrial dysfunction, cellular senescence, stem-cell exhaustion, altered intercellular communication, chronic inflammation, and dysbiosis.<ref name="hallmarks">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2022.11.001 López-Otín, C. et al. (2023), “Hallmarks of Aging: An Expanding Universe,” ''Cell'' 186, 243–278.]</ref>

The framework organizes evidence; it does not mean that every hallmark is independent or equally causal in every organism.

== Senescence is not the whole of aging ==

Cellular senescence is one contributor to aging biology, not a complete explanation.

Likewise:

* telomere length is not a universal biological clock;
* an epigenetic-age estimate is not identical to biological age;
* removing one class of senescent cells would not automatically reverse every aging process.

Therapeutic claims require evidence for the specific intervention, tissue, organism, outcome, and time scale.

== Related concepts ==

Senescence intersects with [[Metabolism]], [[Homeostasis]], [[Genetics]], [[Cell biology]], and [[Adaptation (systems)]], but organismal aging and cellular senescence should remain conceptually distinct.

